<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Global Intentional Homicide Trends</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">A Multi-Dimensional Analysis (1990–2023) — Drawing on UNODC Crime Trends Survey data across 201 countries and territories</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Source: UNODC CTS via Kaggle &bull; 22,092 observations &bull; Report generated April 2026</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPI DASHBOARD -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">2.58</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Global Median Rate</div>
    <div class="kpi-detail">Per 100k pop. (most recent year per country)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card alert">
    <div class="kpi-value">5.9×</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Ratio</div>
    <div class="kpi-detail">Males nearly 6× more likely to be victims</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card warning">
    <div class="kpi-value">76.3</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate (2022)</div>
    <div class="kpi-detail">Turks and Caicos Islands</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card success">
    <div class="kpi-value">−9.6%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">2000s → 2010s Change</div>
    <div class="kpi-detail">Mean rate: 8.51 → 7.70 per 100k</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">53%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Reporting Coverage (2022)</div>
    <div class="kpi-detail">106 of 201 countries reporting</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- TABLE OF CONTENTS -->
<div class="toc">
  <h3>Contents</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#global-overview">Global Homicide Rate Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#regional-comparison">Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</a></li>
    <li><a href="#sex-disparity">Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</a></li>
    <li><a href="#age-group-analysis">Age Group Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#notable-trends">Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</a></li>
    <li><a href="#data-limitations-recommendations">Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</a></li>
  </ol>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: GLOBAL OVERVIEW -->
<section id="global-overview">
  <h2>1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>

  <p>Over the 34-year observation window (1990–2023), the global landscape of intentional homicide reveals a <strong>nuanced trajectory</strong>: while the median homicide rate has declined meaningfully — from approximately 3.96 per 100,000 in the 1990s to 2.35 in the 2020s — the mean rate has been more resistant to decline, reflecting the outsized influence of a small number of extremely high-violence countries.</p>

  <p>Reporting coverage peaked at <strong>162 countries in 2008</strong> (81% of the 201 countries/territories in the dataset), then declined to 84 countries by 2023 (42%). This shrinking coverage in recent years means that global aggregates for the most recent period should be interpreted with caution, as the composition of reporting countries shifts year to year.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartGlobalTrend"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Decade-Level Summary</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Mean Rate</th><th>Median Rate</th><th>Countries</th><th>Observations</th><th>Change vs. Prior</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1990s</td><td>8.27</td><td>3.96</td><td>131</td><td>1,012</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000s</td><td>8.51</td><td>3.33</td><td>182</td><td>1,385</td><td class="worsen">+2.9%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010s</td><td>7.70</td><td>2.62</td><td>186</td><td>1,380</td><td class="improve">−9.6%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020s</td><td>7.71</td><td>2.35</td><td>137</td><td>438</td><td class="worsen">+0.2%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>Key finding:</strong> The 2000s-to-2010s transition saw the most significant improvement (−9.6% in mean rate). The 2020s data so far shows stagnation, though fewer countries are reporting, making direct comparison unreliable.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartCoverage"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p>The gap between the mean and median — consistently large throughout the period — underscores the <strong>skewed distribution</strong> of homicide rates globally. A small number of nations with extremely high rates pull the mean significantly above the median. This asymmetry is a critical consideration for policy: global averages mask the concentration of lethal violence in specific regions and countries.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: REGIONAL COMPARISON -->
<section id="regional-comparison">
  <h2>2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>

  <p>Homicide rates are far from evenly distributed. The Americas — particularly Latin America and the Caribbean — stand apart with rates several times higher than those observed in Europe, Asia, or Oceania. This pattern has persisted throughout the entire observation period.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartRegionalBars"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Regional Trends by Decade</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Region</th><th>1990s Mean</th><th>2000s Mean</th><th>2010s Mean</th><th>2020s Mean</th><th>Trajectory</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Americas</td><td>16.02</td><td>18.14</td><td>19.36</td><td>19.06</td><td><span class="badge badge-danger">Persistently high</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Africa</td><td>12.87</td><td>8.39</td><td>7.22</td><td>7.67</td><td><span class="badge badge-warning">Improved, stalling</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Asia</td><td>4.51</td><td>3.03</td><td>2.33</td><td>1.93</td><td><span class="badge badge-success">Steady decline</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Europe</td><td>4.19</td><td>3.14</td><td>1.91</td><td>1.36</td><td><span class="badge badge-success">Steady decline</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Oceania</td><td>2.24</td><td>3.29</td><td>3.41</td><td>1.50</td><td><span class="badge badge-info">Variable</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-danger">
    <strong>Americas disparity:</strong> The Americas averaged 19.06 per 100k in the 2020s — nearly <strong>14× higher</strong> than Europe (1.36) and <strong>10× higher</strong> than Asia (1.93) in the same period.
  </div>

  <h3>Top 10 Countries by Homicide Rate (2022)</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Rate per 100k</th><th>Region</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Turks and Caicos Islands</td><td class="worsen">76.34</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Jamaica</td><td class="worsen">53.11</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>South Africa</td><td class="worsen">43.72</td><td>Africa</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</td><td class="worsen">41.16</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Trinidad and Tobago</td><td class="worsen">40.44</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>Saint Lucia</td><td class="worsen">36.92</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>Honduras</td><td class="worsen">34.97</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Bahamas</td><td class="worsen">32.20</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>Dominica</td><td class="worsen">28.43</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>Belize</td><td class="worsen">28.06</td><td>Americas</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Bottom 10 Countries by Homicide Rate (2022)</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Rate per 100k</th><th>Region</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Holy See</td><td class="improve">0.00</td><td>Europe</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Qatar</td><td class="improve">0.07</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Singapore</td><td class="improve">0.12</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Bahrain</td><td class="improve">0.20</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Japan</td><td class="improve">0.23</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>Oman</td><td class="improve">0.25</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>Macao</td><td class="improve">0.28</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Indonesia</td><td class="improve">0.30</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>Hong Kong</td><td class="improve">0.40</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>Switzerland</td><td class="improve">0.48</td><td>Europe</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The concentration of the highest-rate countries in the Americas — particularly the Caribbean — is striking. Nine of the ten most dangerous countries or territories in 2022 are in the Americas. The only non-Americas entry is South Africa, which has faced persistent challenges with violent crime. Meanwhile, the safest countries are predominantly East Asian and Gulf states, along with European micro-states.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartRegionalTrend"></canvas>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: GENDER DISPARITY -->
<section id="sex-disparity">
  <h2>3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>

  <p>Gender disparity in intentional homicide is one of the most consistent and stark findings in this dataset. Across all years and regions, <strong>males are overwhelmingly more likely to be victims of intentional homicide than females</strong>.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); margin-bottom: 24px;">
    <div class="kpi-card alert">
      <div class="kpi-value">5.9×</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Overall M/F Ratio</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Mean male rate 13.95 vs. female 2.35</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">3.15</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Median M/F Ratio</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Country-level median (excl. zero-female rates)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card warning">
      <div class="kpi-value">6.4×</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Recent Ratio (2021–23)</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Gap has widened in the most recent data</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartGenderTrend"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p>The mean male homicide rate across all observations is <strong>13.95 per 100,000</strong>, compared to <strong>2.35 per 100,000</strong> for females — a ratio of approximately 5.9:1. This ratio has remained remarkably stable over three decades, with the median country-level ratio hovering around 3.0–3.5 throughout.</p>

  <p>The difference between the mean ratio (5.9) and the median ratio (3.15) reflects the pull of countries with extreme gender disparities. In several high-violence nations, the male rate exceeds the female rate by 10× or more, driven by gang violence, armed conflict, and organized crime that disproportionately affects young men.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartGenderRatio"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <strong>Policy implication:</strong> While females face lower absolute homicide risk, femicide — the gender-motivated killing of women — is often underreported in aggregate statistics. The relatively stable female rate of ~2 per 100k should not be taken to mean that gender-based violence against women is not a priority. The <em>context</em> of female homicide (intimate partner violence, domestic settings) differs fundamentally from male homicide (street violence, gang-related), requiring distinct policy responses.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: AGE GROUP ANALYSIS -->
<section id="age-group-analysis">
  <h2>4. Age Group Analysis</h2>

  <p>Homicide risk is not uniformly distributed across age groups. The data reveals a clear <strong>age-risk curve</strong> that peaks sharply in young adulthood, with males aged 25–29 facing the highest average rate globally.</p>

