  <div class="callout callout-danger">
    <strong>Critical finding:</strong> Males aged 25–29 face a mean homicide rate of <strong>29.4 per 100,000</strong> — roughly <strong>35× higher</strong> than the rate for children under 10 (0.83) and <strong>9× higher</strong> than females of the same age group. The rate escalates dramatically between ages 15–17 (8.74) and 18–19 (20.33), suggesting that the transition to legal adulthood coincides with a massive spike in lethal violence exposure.
  </div>

  <p>For females, the age profile is flatter: rates rise from approximately 0.5 per 100k in childhood to a peak of about 3.5 at ages 18–19, declining only slightly through middle age. This flatter curve likely reflects the different drivers of female homicide — domestic and intimate partner violence affects women across age groups, unlike the street and gang violence concentrated among young men.</p>

  <p>The gender ratio itself shifts dramatically with age. Among children 0–9, the M/F ratio is only 1.5:1 — close to parity. By ages 25–29, it reaches 9.1:1. This widening gap underscores that the mechanisms driving male homicide in young adulthood (organized crime, territorial disputes, firearms access) are qualitatively different from those affecting children and older adults.</p>
</section>

