<!-- SECTION 6: DATA LIMITATIONS & POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>

  <p>The findings in this report must be interpreted in light of several significant data constraints:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Limitation</th><th>Impact</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Unbalanced panel</strong></td>
        <td>Not all countries report every year; the set of reporting countries changes annually. This means "global" averages reflect different country compositions year to year, potentially biasing trend estimates.</td>
        <td>We report median alongside mean to reduce outlier influence, and note the number of reporting countries with each statistic.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Declining coverage (2020s)</strong></td>
        <td>Coverage dropped from 162 countries in 2008 to 84 in 2023 (42%). The 2020s data should not be directly compared to the 2000s or 2010s without accounting for this compositional shift.</td>
        <td>We focus decade comparisons on direction rather than precise magnitudes, and explicitly flag the coverage rate as a KPI.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>UK data caveat</strong></td>
        <td>United Kingdom data refers to England and Wales only. Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries, but are not combined into a single UK figure.</td>
        <td>Users should be aware when using country-level UK comparisons.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Territory inclusion</strong></td>
        <td>Territories (Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico, overseas French departments, etc.) are included as separate entries. Risk of double-counting with parent countries.</td>
        <td>Territory status is noted where relevant; small-population territories are flagged when they appear as extreme outliers.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>No imputation</strong></td>
        <td>Missing years are left absent — not interpolated. This preserves data integrity but means trend lines have gaps for many countries.</td>
        <td>All reported figures are directly from the source data. No values were fabricated or imputed.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Sex/age disaggregation gaps</strong></td>
        <td>Not all countries report sex- or age-disaggregated data, reducing sample size for the gender and age analyses.</td>
        <td>We report sample sizes alongside all disaggregated statistics.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>1. Prioritize interventions in the Americas and Caribbean.</strong> With mean rates of 19+ per 100k — an order of magnitude above Europe and Asia — the Americas region accounts for a disproportionate share of global homicide. Caribbean island states in particular face escalating violence that outpaces their institutional capacity. International cooperation on drug trafficking disruption, weapons control, and community violence intervention programs should be concentrated here.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>2. Target young males aged 15–29 with prevention programs.</strong> This demographic faces by far the highest risk, with male 25–29 rates averaging 29.4 per 100k. Gang diversion, employment, education, and mentorship programs should be priority investments in high-violence countries.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>3. Invest in reporting infrastructure.</strong> The decline in global reporting coverage to 42% by 2023 undermines the ability to monitor progress and allocate resources. Capacity building for national crime data systems — particularly in Africa and parts of Asia — is essential for evidence-based policy.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>4. Address gender-specific violence through differentiated strategies.</strong> The 6:1 male-to-female ratio reflects fundamentally different violence dynamics. Male homicide prevention requires addressing gang recruitment, firearms access, and organized crime. Female homicide prevention requires addressing domestic violence, intimate partner violence, and legal protections for women.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>5. Study and replicate success stories.</strong> El Salvador (−94%), Colombia (−67%), and several post-Soviet states achieved dramatic reductions. While each context is unique, comparative analysis of successful interventions can inform strategies elsewhere.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>6. Monitor emerging crises.</strong> Ecuador, Haiti, and several Caribbean nations show rapid deterioration in recent years. Early warning systems and rapid-response frameworks should be established to detect and respond to surging homicide rates before they become entrenched.
  </div>
</section>

